findings.description:
The 900 architecture lists non-trivial bug fixes and operational logging as in scope, and asks for small, independently deliverable slices. All three things the plan assigns are present:
- the re-enterability decision (D1);
- the resume fix (Parts A and B);
- observable abandonment (Part C).

Nothing from the plan entry is dropped or deferred. The 910 and 911 dependencies are declared. The A → C → B sequencing with a separate commit for Part A is sensible for bisecting.

After D1, a checkpoint pause inside a loop is not abandonment. The loop is re-entered on resume. Part C still logs at WARNING, naming "rounds not run", on every legitimate pause. That is noise, and it conflicts with the doc's own framing. The WARNING is also not tied to the paths where rounds really are dropped:
- the iteration-beyond-`max` case above;
- a non-loop target ignoring the coordinate;
- a run abandoned at a pause and never resumed.

The plan does require abandoned iterations to be observable "regardless of which answer is chosen", so Part C stays. Revise it so the pause-time message reads as a paused-with-pending-rounds notice, and put the WARNING on the actual drop paths. The resume-time INFO line is not a failure-mode signal. It is also absent from the Success Criteria, so it can be cut or listed there.
